            <date type="death" when="1939-06-26"/>
            <note type="gender">M</note>
         </ab>
         <div type="bio">
            <p>Writer and editor. Especially with his best known novel, <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">The Good Soldier</name> (1915), Ford made an important contribution to literary Modernism. He met Joyce for the first time thanks to <name type="person" key="poun85">Ezra Pound</name>, who organized a personal meeting in late November 1922 (see Joyce’s letter to <name type="person" key="weav76">Harriet Shaw Weaver</name> of 25 November 1922; <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">LIII</name> 69). In 1924, Ford founded the <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">transatlantic review</name>, whose April 1924 issue contained the first pre-book publication of <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Finnegans Wake</name> (the MaMaLuJo episode) under the title “From Work in Progress”. After that, “Work in Progress” was to remain the working title of what eventually became <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Finnegans Wake</name>. <hi rend="bold">Dirk Van Hulle</hi> and <hi rend="bold">William Brockman</hi>
                </p>
         </div>
